Infinite's own source code is licensed under the MIT License above.

By default, the build links the Steinberg VST3 SDK (`INFINITE_ENABLE_VST3=ON`),
which is licensed under the GNU General Public License version 3 (GPLv3). The
distributed binary of a default build must therefore be distributed under the
terms of the GPLv3. Building with `-DINFINITE_ENABLE_VST3=OFF` excludes the VST3
SDK and the resulting binary remains under the plain MIT terms above.

The Windows build bundles `assets/models/u2netp.onnx`, the small U^2-Net
variant's weights, for on-device background removal (macOS uses Vision
instead and does not ship this file). Those weights are from
https://github.com/xuebinqin/U-2-Net and are licensed under the Apache
License 2.0; see `assets/models/NOTICE.txt`. The Windows build also links
ONNX Runtime (MIT) and, for GPU acceleration, the `Microsoft.AI.DirectML`
NuGet redistributable, under its own license terms - see CMakeLists.txt and
the license file NuGet stages alongside it.

On Linux, by default (`INFINITE_ENABLE_GPL_CODECS=ON`), the build links a
prebuilt FFmpeg compiled with `--enable-gpl` and libx264 for H.264 video
encoding (`Platform::RecorderStart` in `src/platform/linux/MediaLinux.cpp`).
FFmpeg's own licensing already becomes GPL the moment `--enable-gpl` is
turned on, and libx264 is GPLv2-or-later, so a default Linux build is GPLv3
for this reason as well as (from P4 onward) the VST3 SDK reason above - the
two reasons overlap rather than stack, since either alone already makes the
combined work GPLv3. This is not new copyleft exposure for the project (see
`docs/plans/linux/phase-03-media.md`'s "Licence decision" for the full
reasoning); it does mean video recording and camera capture, unlike on macOS
and Windows, involve a bundled software H.264 encoder, and this project is
consequently a distributor of one for the first time. See
`THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES` for exact FFmpeg/x264 versions, source pins and
checksums, and where their licence texts ship inside the Linux build.

Building with `-DINFINITE_ENABLE_GPL_CODECS=OFF` on Linux links LGPL FFmpeg
instead (no `--enable-gpl`, no x264 linked in), for distros (Fedora, RHEL,
Flathub's main repo) that cannot ship a bundled H.264 encoder. That
configuration produces no video encoding capability. Combined with
`-DINFINITE_ENABLE_VST3=OFF`, it is the only Linux build configuration that
stays under the plain MIT terms above.
